// cffi U3 — the `void` RESULT (`FVoid`).
//
// The claim FVoid has to hold is a NEGATIVE one: a declared `-> void` must move
// NOTHING.  Today's undeclared `rawcall free(p)` already emits `%t =l call`
// with a dead word (plan §3.1), and a `-> void` declaration has to emit that
// same shape — `=l call`, no extension, no `w` result class.
//
// So this fixture puts the two shapes ADJACENT in one emitted defun:
//
//   word p = rawcall malloc(8);   ->  %t0 =l call $malloc(l 8)     (legacy: rawcallArity row)
//   rawcall srand(7);             ->  %t1 =l call $srand(w 7)      (DECLARED `-> void`)
//   rawcall free(p);              ->  %t2 =l call $free(l %t0)     (legacy: same symbol class)
//
// and qbe-check.sh's run_midshape row pins the DECLARED line with the EMPTY
// extension op — the arm that FAILS if the line after the call extends the
// result, which is what a `-> void` emitted through the `w`-plus-extension
// path would do.  The legacy `free` line beside it is the control that says the
// shape is the same one the corpus already contains.
//
// WHY `srand` AND NOT `free`: `free` (like `close` and `_exit`) is a
// `rawcallArity` row, and R-08 refuses to declare a symbol that already has a
// table row — the declaration would be a SECOND source of truth for its arity.
// Deleting the row is cffi U5's migration (it would break every unmigrated
// `rawcall free` site, runtime.mid's included).  `srand` is a real libc `void`
// function with no row, which is exactly what a first declaration needs.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT SHOW, stated rather than implied: a `void` call has
// no result to get wrong, so the GOLDEN is inert with respect to FVoid — it
// only proves the program runs end to end with the call in the middle.  The
// instrument that can FAIL is the shape pin, plus `tools/osier-corpus-ssa.sh`'s
// byte-identity over the corpus (which contains runtime.mid's own `rawcall
// free` sites) for the "moves nothing" half.
module Ffiv
export Ffiv.run : val -> val

extern srand(seed : u32) -> void;

defun Ffiv.run(dummy : val) -> val {
  word p = rawcall malloc(8);
  rawcall srand(7);
  rawcall free(p);
  return mkbool(1 == 1);
}
